<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ibrahim II of Karaman</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1464">1464</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Karamanoğlu İbrahim Bey</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ibrahim II was ruler of the Karamanid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a> in south-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> from 1424 to 1464. He fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> repeatedly, often allied with crusaders and other Turkish rivals, and built mosques and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karaman">Karaman</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a>. After his death his sons fought over the succession, opening the way for the Ottoman conquest.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ibrahim-ii-of-karaman.jpg" alt="Ibrahim II of Karaman" width="320" height="184" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gold_dirhem_Ibrahim_II_Karamanid.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The Karamanids, centred on the present-day provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karaman">Karaman</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a>, had been the strongest of the Anatolian beyliks that arose after the Seljuk era, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> came to dominate most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> late in the 14th century. Ibrahim, born in 1400, was the son of Mehmet Bey. He fought his uncle Ali Bey for the throne and took it in 1424 with Ottoman support. In 1425 he married Ilaldi Sultan Hatun, a daughter of the Ottoman sultan Mehmed I; the marriage was unhappy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Ottoman help did not buy his loyalty. He secretly allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beysehir">Beyşehir</a> while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> were at war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. When Sultan Murat II returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> in 1433, Ibrahim sued for peace in 1435, then besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amasya">Amasya</a>; Murat backed the Dulkadirids in taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayseri">Kayseri</a> and Ibrahim's brother İsa in taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksehir">Akşehir</a>, and Ibrahim lifted the siege in 1437. In 1444, as a crusader army marched on the Ottoman capital, he plundered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutahya">Kütahya</a>. Murat signed the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szeged">Szeged</a> with the crusaders, turned on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karaman">Karaman</a> and forced Ibrahim into an unfavourable treaty.</p><p>Relations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> were usually good, though they had backed İsa against him early on. Rivalry over Çukurova, ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a>, ended the friendship. In 1448 Ibrahim took the castle of Corycus, modern Kızkalesi, from the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, which opened the way to the rest of Çukurova, but in 1456 the Mamluks and their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> beylik of Ramazan, attacked him, and he gave up hope of conquering the region. After Murat's death he allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, but did not fight the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In his last years his sons fought for the throne. His chosen heir was Ishak, governor of Silifke, but the younger Pir Ahmed proclaimed himself sultan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a>. Ibrahim and Ishak fled to Gevale Castle near Konya, and Ibrahim died as they reached it. One account, given by Şikârî and supported by Kıvâmî, holds that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a>, promising him the rule of the Karamanid lands, induced Ibrahim's own vizier Ahmed Pasha to poison him with a cluster of grapes, and that Ibrahim died 26 days later; another says he died of natural causes.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ibrahim II of Karaman” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
